  "step_2": "The individual reports that they first looked at both options. They mention that the expected value calculation was complex in their head, so they repeatedly reviewed the probabilities and outcomes. Then, they computed the expected value mentally and concluded that Option 2 (which corresponds to Lottery B) is slightly better than Option 1 (Lottery A). The individual does not mention skewness, probability distribution shape, or any consideration of unfavorable vs. favorable outcomes in terms of probability mass. The justification is entirely based on expected value calculation: they computed the expected gains and found B slightly better. There is no reference to skewness, right-skewness, or any discussion about the distribution of outcomes being more or less skewed. The deliberation process is focused on expected value, not on the shape of the probability distribution or asymmetry of outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the more right-skewed lottery — a lottery where unfavorable outcomes (0 Euros) have a greater probability mass. In this case, both lotteries have high probability (67% and 66%) on the 0 Euro outcome, and low probability (33% and 34%) on the positive outcome. Thus, both are actually left-skewed (mass on the left), not right-skewed. There is no right-skewed lottery here, so the reason cannot be applied. The individual's justification is entirely based on expected value calculation, not on skewness. There is no mention of comparing the distribution shapes or the probability mass on unfavorable outcomes. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The reasoning used is completely different — expected value — and not aligned with the skewness rule specified in the decision reason. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

